## ⚡ Quick Start: Local Setup (PC & Mac)

### 1. Clone Repository & Install Dependencies
```bash
git clone [https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/toyota-nav.git](https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/toyota-nav.git)
cd toyota-nav
npm install

2. Start Dev Server for Local Network Access
Bash

npm run dev -- --host

    On your PC: Open http://localhost:5173

    On Android Devices (Same Wi-Fi): Open http://<YOUR_PC_IP>:5173

📱 Running on Android Devices
Method 1: Web Browser (Instant Testing)

    Open Chrome on your Android phone/tablet.

    In the URL bar, go to: chrome://flags/#unsafely-treat-insecure-origin-as-secure

    Set the flag to Enabled.

    In the text box, type your laptop's address: http://<YOUR_PC_IP>:5173

    Tap Relaunch.

    Navigate to http://<YOUR_PC_IP>:5173, click LOCK REAL GPS, and select Allow when prompted for location access.

Method 2: Standalone Native APK (Cloud Build via GitHub)

    Commit and push your code to GitHub:
    Bash

    git add .
    git commit -m "feat: release build"
    git push origin main

    Go to your repository on GitHub and click the Actions tab.

    Select the latest Build Android APK workflow run.

    Download the Toyota-OS-Debug-APK artifact at the bottom of the page.

    Transfer the .apk to your phone/head unit and install.

Method 3: Build APK Locally via Terminal
Bash

npm run build
npx cap sync android
cd android && ./gradlew assembleDebug

Output location: android/app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk
🗺️ Offline City Maps: Complete MOBAC Guide (All OS)

To enable 100% offline navigation for your specific city or region, use Mobile Atlas Creator (MOBAC) to export local map tiles.
1. Install Java

    Linux (Ubuntu/Debian):
    Bash

    sudo apt update && sudo apt install -y openjdk-17-jre

    Windows: Download and install Java 17 from adoptium.net.

2. Download & Run MOBAC

    Download the MOBAC zip archive from mobac.sourceforge.io.

    Extract the archive:

        Linux: Run java -jar Mobile_Atlas_Creator.jar

        Windows: Double-click Mobile_Atlas_Creator.exe

3. Configure Tile Export Format

    When prompted for atlas format, select OSMTracker tile storage and click OK.
    (If not prompted, go to Atlas settings -> New -> choose OSMTracker tile storage).

4. Select Map Region & Detail Levels

    Under Map source (top-left), select OpenStreetMap Standard.

    Pan and zoom to your target city on the map canvas.

    Hold Right-Click and drag a bounding box across your city.

    Under Zoom levels, check the boxes for:

        [x] 14 (City overview)

        [x] 15 (Districts)

        [x] 16 (Neighborhoods)

        [x] 17 (Streets & main lanes)

        [x] 18 (Detailed building outlines)

5. Generate & Place Tiles

    Under Atlas Content, enter a name (e.g., CityMap) and click Add selection.

    Click Create atlas at the bottom-left.

    Once completed, click Open Atlas Folder.

    Copy the numbered folders (14, 15, 16, 17, 18) directly into your project's public/tiles/ directory:

Plaintext

toyota-nav/
└── public/
    └── tiles/
        ├── 14/
        ├── 15/
        ├── 16/
        ├── 17/
        └── 18/
            └── <x>/
                └── <y>.png

6. Verification

Turn off Wi-Fi and Mobile Data on your device. Launch the app—the UI will switch to OFFLINE MODE and render your local city tiles directly from storage.
🔒 Security, Privacy & Permissions Notice

    Satellite Location (ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION): Geolocation coordinates are processed strictly in-memory on the device for real-time speed calculation, route positioning, and map centering. No location telemetry is stored or sent to remote servers.

    Telephony Gateway (CALL_PHONE): Initiates calls via standard Android system intents (tel:). Does not access contact books or read call logs.

    Public APIs: Reverse geocoding (Nominatim), route geometry calculation (OSRM), and ambient weather temperature (Open-Meteo) run via public HTTPS endpoints
